      "source_example_id": "e-14614",
      "knowledge_point": "Representing Binary Relations Using Digraphs",
      "kp_id": "e-14614"
    },
    "q-212120": {
      "dom_id": "step-q212120",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14120255/assets/847bcee3b9f9fc6672d5858111866141e043e390605cd92fb5579fd5193d7df4.png)\n\nThe directed graph of a relation defined on the set ${1,2,3}$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\n- $(2,1)$\n- $(3,3)$\n- $(2,3)$",
      "worked_solution": "A binary relation can be visualized using a directed graph (digraph), where \n\n- the nodes (vertices) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an arrow from node $x$ to node $y$,\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a loop from node $x$ to itself.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(2,1)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has an arrow that starts at the node $2$ and ends at the node $1.$\n\n- The pair $(3,3)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has a loop from the node $3$ to itself.\n\n- The pair $(2,3)$ does not belong to the relation since the directed graph does not have an arrow that starts at the node $2$ and ends at the node $3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-212120-a",
